    description: "Partial-install / linker-requirement diagnostics must count only first-party implementations (tiers 1-2), not tier-3 vendored code (ADR-0004 3a, amendment item). Today on hypergumbo itself they fire twice on vendored tree-sitter grammar C under packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/vendor/:\n  \"CGO-LINKER linker found 53 C/C++ function implementations but 0 Go cgo calls\"\n  \"RUBY-FFI-LINKER linker found 53 C/C++ function implementations but 0 Ruby files\"\n\nOrdering bug to fix first: the check (check_partial_install_warnings, called at cli.py ~12825; logic in partial_install_warnings.py ~421-551) runs BEFORE _classify_symbols (cli.py ~12850), so symbols carry no tier yet. Move it after classification, keeping linker_ctx. Then count requirements on tier <= 2 symbols (the C/C++ count comes from linkers/cgo.py ~84 and siblings). Leave the actual linking untouched -- linkers still link into vendored code.\n\nEvidence at close: a self-survey prints neither warning; a fixture with first-party C and no Go still prints the CGO warning (the warning is not simply deleted). Related, distinct: WI-tonuh (requirement-table key mismatch in the same module), WI-vadom (stderr noise umbrella)."  # 7cf5
